      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single, universally agreed-upon \"definitive\" moral framework that justifies preemptive strikes by democratic nations against authoritarian regimes globally, as ethics in international law (like the UN Charter) strictly prohibit such action without explicit security threats. While utilitarianism might argue actions are justifiable if they prevent greater future suffering, deontology often condemns unprovoked aggression regardless of outcomes. Ultimately, most political scientists would conclude that for an action to be morally defensible, there must be immediate evidence of an imminent threat rather than a vague fear of future authoritarian rise.",
